## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an EU-resident analytics flow with data governance, simple serverless components, and cost awareness.

## Key Concepts
- EU residency and data sovereignty
- Event-driven ingestion with Azure Event Hubs
- Serverless processing with Azure Functions
- Per-tenant access control via Cosmos DB RLS
- BYOK using Azure Key Vault
- Data retention and purge workflows
- DR planning and cost optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
module.exports = async function (context, eventHubMessages) {
  const { CosmosClient } = require('@azure/cosmos');
  const client = new CosmosClient(process.env.COSMOS_CONNECTION);
  const container = client.database('Analytics').container('TenantStats');
  for (const msg of eventHubMessages) {
    const payload = JSON.parse(msg.body);
    const item = {
      id: payload.id,
      tenantId: payload.tenantId,
      metric: payload.metric,
      value: payload.value,
      ts: new Date().toISOString()
    };
    await container.items.upsert(item);
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle GDPR data deletion requests in this flow?
- What tests would you run to verify retention and DR requirements?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates comprehensive data pipeline design capabilities using Azure's native services, requiring candidates to demonstrate architectural thinking while maintaining accessibility for beginners. It tests understanding of end-to-end data flows, governance principles, and enterprise requirements.

## Key Concepts
- IoT Hub for telemetry ingestion
- Data Lake Storage Gen2 for scalable raw data storage
- Azure Synapse Analytics for data processing and aggregation
- Azure Purview for metadata management and data governance
- Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) for multi-tenant isolation
- Power BI for data visualization and business intelligence
- Bring Your Own Key (BYOK) for enhanced encryption
- Paired region disaster recovery architecture
- Serverless compute and storage tiering for cost optimization

